course: dictionary
lesson: 8199
title: Result: the end, the cause and the score
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word result has more than one meaning, so read the words around it to find the right one. Ask what came before it and what came after it.
explain: Result can mean the answer at the end of something, like the answer to a sum. It can also mean what happens because of something else: «As a result of the rain, the game stopped.» Here the rain came first, and the stopping came after it.
explain: Results can also be the scores or marks from a test, game or race. «Cheating spoils a game, because nobody can trust the result.» The result here is the final score, and cheating makes that score unfair.
fact: Result is a noun. Results is its plural, so one result and two or more results.
fact: The verb to result means to cause something: rain can result in a flooded field.
fact: «As a result» means because of something that came before.
fact: In a fair test, you change only one thing, so if the result changes, you know what caused it.
fact: Scores, marks and final answers are all kinds of results.
exercise: In a fair test, what is the result? | The person who ran the test | *What you find out, written down in a table | The equipment you use | A rule you must never break
exercise: «As a result of the rain, the game stopped.» What does as a result mean here? | *Because of the rain | Before the rain started | After the game had ended | Without the rain
exercise: «Cheating spoils a game, because nobody can trust the result.» What is the result here? | The rules of the game | The team's kit | The name of the referee | *The final score
test: «Results of blood tests can take days or weeks.» What are the results here? | *The findings that come back from the tests | The things you need to bring to the clinic | The people who work in the lab | A kind of medicine
test: Which word means the answer you get at the end of a sum? | *Result | Fair | Scan | Cause
exam: «And if your clay owl cracks, you show it anyway, because the work and what you learned count, not only a perfect result.» In this sentence, what does result mean? | A small crack in the clay | *The final outcome, such as how good the finished owl is | A letter from the teacher | A type of clay
